# .trivyignore — reviewed and accepted CVE allowlist
#
# This file is the suppression list for every Trivy gate in the project. All
# three run the SAME policy — a fixable CRITICAL is a finding
# (`severity: CRITICAL`, `ignore-unfixed: true`) — and differ only in what a
# finding COSTS:
#
#   - .github/workflows/docker-publish.yml, the "Trivy gate" step of the
#     `scan-containers` job. BLOCKING. It runs once per published image and
#     architecture, and `merge` depends on it, so a hit here is what stands
#     between a CRITICAL and a held ghcr publish.
#   - .gitlab-ci.yml `build-images:hort-server` / `build-images:hort-worker`,
#     which scan the image they just built BEFORE its first push. BLOCKING:
#     the image is one hort produces, the finding is ours to fix, and it must
#     not reach the registry or receive a cosign signature.
#   - .gitlab-ci.yml `security:base-image-scan`, which scans the digest-pinned
#     base images on every code-changing push. WARNING for a CVE finding
#     (`allow_failure: exit_codes: [2]`): a base image's CRITICAL is cleared
#     by an upstream rebuild, which no change in this repository can produce.
#     It still FAILS HARD when the scan did not run — a refused pull, a
#     database that would not download, a scanner error — because that is this
#     pipeline breaking rather than an upstream CVE.
#
# So an entry here is not how a base-image finding stops blocking: it does not
# block. It is for the two gates that do, and for silencing a base-image
# warning the maintainer has decided to stop being told about.
#
# The last two reach trivy through scripts/ci/trivy-image-gate.sh, which
# prints this file's active entries before it scans anything: a suppressed
# finding is a visible decision in the job log, never a silent one.
#
# ONE list, deliberately, rather than a per-gate list: an accepted CVE that
# reached only the publish gate would leave every development push red — or
# permanently yellow — for a finding the maintainer has already ruled on, and
# a gate that is always the same colour is a gate people learn to ignore.
#
# List one CVE ID per line (e.g. `CVE-2024-12345`). A line here means the CVE
# has been reviewed and consciously accepted — typically an unfixable
# base-image CVE Trivy still flags as fixable, or a CVE in a code path the
# image does not exercise.
#
# Discipline:
#   - Every addition MUST be reviewed (a CVE silenced here is a CVE we ship).
#   - Prefer upgrading the affected layer/dependency over adding an entry.
#   - Keep a short justification comment above each ID and reassess on each
#     release so stale acceptances do not accumulate.
#
# (Empty by default — no CVEs are currently accepted.)
